sourceReview.sourceQuestion:
The diagram above shows the curve $y=e^{ax}$.
The tangent to the curve at the point where $x=b$ meets the $x$-axis at $T$.
Note that $a$ is a positive real number.

[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2, every node/.style={font=\large}]
    % Axes (Note: the original PDF has arrows on both ends of the axes here)
    \draw[<->, thick] (-4,0) -- (5,0) node[below] {$x$};
    \draw[<->, thick] (0,-2) -- (0,4) node[left] {$y$};

    % Exponential curve (using 0.6*e^{0.7x} for visual proportion)
    \draw[thick, <->, domain=-3.8:2.5, samples=100] plot (\x, {0.6*exp(0.7*\x)});
    \node at (-2, 1) {$y=e^{ax}$};

    % Coordinates for Tangency
    \coordinate (P) at (2, 2.43); % Tangent point at b=2
    \coordinate (T) at (0.57, 0); % x-intercept of the tangent

    % Tangent line
    \draw[thick, <->] (-0.5, -1.82) -- (2.8, 3.79);

    % Points and dashed dropping line
    \filldraw (P) circle (1.5pt);
    \filldraw (T) circle (1.5pt) node[below right] {$T$};
    \draw[dashed, thin] (P) -- (2,0) node[below] {$b$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

$\textbf{(i)}$ Find the coordinates of $T$ in terms of $a$ and $b$.
[3]
$\textbf{(ii)}$ Hence, explain why any straight line which passes through a given point $(p, 0)$, and which cuts the curve $y=e^{ax}$ twice, has gradient greater than $ae^{ap+1}$.
[2]

